```
kk: Work around zero-extended 32-bit shift miscompile

Works around a Metal compiler bug that makes some shaders read and write
the wrong memory. Reported to Apple as FB25499479 on 2026-11-09, no
feedback so far.

## The bug

Metal miscompiles `zext(shl i32 x, c)` used as a 64-bit address offset
when the top bits of `x` are set: the shifted-out bits are kept, as if
the shift was done in 64 bits. The access lands outside the buffer, loads
return 0 and stores are dropped without a fault. Masking the value in
the shader does not help, the front end folds it back. Seen on M5 Pro,
in Enshrouded (black global illumination and shadows). Not caught by
the CTS. A standalone repro is available if Apple or reviewers want it.

## The workaround

Xor the shared 32-bit base of each affected address with an opaque zero
loaded from the root descriptor table (`dynamic_buffers[0].zero`), so the
backend no longer sees the shift and keeps the math in 32 bits. Internal
shaders (`kk_clc`, `kosmicomp`) rebuild the tree in 64 bits and mask it
instead. `MESA_KK_WA21_FORM=widen` selects that form for the driver.
It is workaround 21 because 20 is already the OOM guard in `kk_bo.c`.
Disable with `MESA_KK_DISABLE_WORKAROUNDS=21`. It is documented in
`workarounds.rst` and can be dropped once Apple fixes the bug.

## Testing

- `msl_compiler_tests`: 15/15 pass (new `test_widen_u2u64_shifts`).
- CTS r1c: 0 new failures against plain upstream.
- All 2,754 compute modules of a shader corpus compile.
- Cost on Enshrouded, plain upstream, 3 rounds: -0.52% mean FPS, -0.67%
  1% low. That is the price of the cor
